<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prague Spring</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> Spring was the period of reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> that began when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a> became party leader in January 1968. Censorship was relaxed and the party's Action Programme promised wider freedoms and economic reform, socialism with a human face. Alarmed, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and its allies invaded on the night of 20–21 August 1968 and ended the reforms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prague-spring.jpg" alt="Prague Spring" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Central Intelligence Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:10_Soviet_Invasion_of_Czechoslovakia_-_Flickr_-_The_Central_Intelligence_Agency.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>De-Stalinization in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> began under Antonín Novotný in the late 1950s and early 1960s but moved more slowly than in most other countries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a>. In the early 1960s the economy declined, and Novotný's New Economic Model of 1965 increased the demand for political reform. In May 1963 the Liblice Conference on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-kafka">Franz Kafka</a> marked the beginning of a cultural opening, and at the Writers' Congress of June 1967 speeches by Ludvík Vaculík, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-kundera">Milan Kundera</a> and Antonín Liehm broke with the regime. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>, First Secretary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, and the economist Ota Šik challenged Novotný in the Central Committee, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a> supported his removal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Dubček replaced Novotný as First Secretary on 5 January 1968, and on 22 March Ludvík Svoboda became president. The writers' weekly became Literární listy and appeared without censorship on 29 February. In April the party launched an Action Programme that promised more freedom of the press, speech and movement, a limit on the power of the secret police, a federation of two equal nations and a ten-year transition towards democratic elections, under the slogan socialism with a human face. Reform was to proceed under the party's direction. Soviet and other leaders were alarmed: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 23 March the leaders of the USSR, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> questioned the Czechoslovak delegation.</p><p>On the night of 20–21 August 1968 armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> countries invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and by the morning of 21 August the country was occupied. During the invasion 72 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> and Slovaks were killed. Dubček called on his people not to resist, but there was widespread nonviolent resistance: road signs were removed or painted over, except those pointing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Dubček was arrested and taken to Moscow, where he and the other leaders except František Kriegel signed the Moscow Protocol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In April 1969 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a> replaced Dubček as First Secretary, and a period called normalisation began. Husák reversed the reforms, purged liberals from the party and re-centralised the economy. The only significant change to survive was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federalism">federalisation</a> of the country into the Czech Socialist Republic and the Slovak Socialist Republic in 1969. On 16 January 1969 the student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-palach">Jan Palach</a> set himself on fire in Wenceslas Square in protest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> remained a Soviet satellite state until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution</a> of 1989, and the last Soviet troops left in 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prague Spring” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 1960 constitution renamed the state the Czechoslovak Socialist Republic, and from 1 January 1969 it became a federation of Czech and Slovak republics, the one reform of the <b>Prague Spring</b> that survived.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The party had bloodily ended a protest in 1970 with machine-gun fire, killing over thirty people and injuring over 1,000, and Soviet-led invasions had crushed the Hungarian Uprising of 1956 and the <b>Prague Spring</b> of 1968.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>People were blacklisted for being children of former entrepreneurs or non-Communist politicians, for having family in the West, for having supported Alexander Dubček during the <b>Prague Spring</b> of 1968, for promoting religion or for signing Charter 77.</blockquote><a href="/movement/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Hundreds of thousands of Soviet-led troops crossed into Czechoslovakia overnight to end Alexander Dubček's <b>Prague Spring</b> reforms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-20">20 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His reforms, 'socialism with a human face', inspired the <b>Prague Spring</b>, ended by a Warsaw Pact invasion in August.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
